  "notes": [
    "Water temperature: Darjeeling leaves are more delicate than typical black teas; brewing with water heated just below a rolling boil (around 85°C–95°C / 185°F–203°F) prevents scorching the leaves and reduces bitter astringency.",
    "Brewing ratio: A standard ratio is 2 to 2.5 grams (approximately 1 heaped teaspoon) of loose leaf per 200–250 ml (8 oz) of water.",
    "Serving style: Unlike bolder, malty black teas (such as Assam), Darjeeling is traditionally enjoyed straight or with a light slice of lemon, as heavy milk and sugar tend to overwhelm its signature floral and muscatel fruit notes.",
    "Botanical type: Unlike most Indian black teas made from the broad-leaf *assamica* variety, Darjeeling is produced from high-altitude *Camellia sinensis* var. *sinensis*, resulting in a lighter body and a more nuanced aromatic profile.",
    "Steeping equipment: Whole and long-leaf teas expand substantially in hot water; steeping them in a spacious basket infuser or an open teapot rather than a cramped mesh ball gives the leaves room to fully unfurl and extract evenly."
